[{:db/id -1, :adr/id "adr-2605181100-mst-encrypted-records-signal-keywrap", :adr/title "ADR-2605181100: MST encrypted records + Signal key-wrap (Tahoe-pattern confidentiality on AT Protocol substrate)", :adr/status "proposed", :adr/doc_type "adr", :adr/source-format "edn", :adr/body "\n# ADR-2605181100: MST encrypted records + Signal key-wrap (Tahoe-pattern confidentiality on AT Protocol substrate)\n\n**Status**: proposed\n**Date**: 2026-05-18\n**Deciders**: Jun Kawasaki\n\n# Context\n\nADR-2605172000 made the etzhayyim substrate **verifiable from outside any single operator** by mandating AT Protocol MST + IPFS + Base L2 as the only durable state. The unintended side-effect: every record is **publicly readable** by anyone who can fetch the PDS repo. That is correct for ADRs, the constitution, public membership roster, and open-* data wrappers, but it makes private communication and member-only artifacts impossible on the substrate as defined.\n\nThree concrete needs surface immediately:\n\n1. **評議会 (council) deliberations** — discussion among 信者 (per ADR-2605172700) that should be visible only to council members, with auditable trail for governance later.\n2. **DM between members** — 1:1 messaging that cannot be deanonymized by the PDS operator.\n3. **Private artifacts** — financial records, medical notes for uhl-right-neural patient referrals (ADR-2605181040–1060), private constitution drafts before publication.\n\nThree implementation paths were evaluated:\n\n- **A. Tahoe-LAFS sidecar** — separate capability-encrypted storage. Forces a second substrate; loses AT Proto verifier + L2 anchor properties; not kotoba in spirit (operators still trust the Tahoe grid operator).\n- **B. MLS group messaging (RFC 9420)** — IETF-standardized successor to Signal's group protocol. Stronger group scaling (O(log N) sender ops) and tree-based forward secrecy. Rejected for v1 because production-grade implementations (`openmls`, AWS MLS) have <2 years of widespread use; the operator-side risk of subtle protocol bugs at our scale is unacceptable.\n- **C. AT Proto MST records encrypted at rest + Signal-wrapped per-record keys** — adds confidentiality to the existing substrate without replacing it. This ADR's choice.\n\nPath C is the **Tahoe pattern on AT Proto**: AT Proto MST already provides Tahoe's verify-cap for free (Merkle proofs to the L2-anchored root); we only need to add the confidentiality envelope and key-distribution mechanism that Tahoe ships in a single package.\n\nSignal protocol (`libsignal-protocol`, used by Signal Messenger, WhatsApp, Google RCS, Meta Messenger) has ~12 years of production deployment, formal cryptographic analysis (X3DH, Double Ratchet), and Rust-native bindings (`@signalapp/libsignal-client`) suitable for the etzhayyim TS stack. At etzhayyim's expected scale (low thousands of members) Signal's O(N) group-send cost is irrelevant; MLS becomes relevant only above ~10⁴ active group members.\n\n# Decision\n\n## Architecture (Tahoe-on-MST)\n\n| Tahoe-LAFS concept | etzhayyim-on-AT-Proto implementation |\n|---|---|\n| **verify-cap** | CID + MST proof to L2-anchored root (already free from ADR-2605172000) |\n| **read-cap** | XChaCha20-Poly1305 symmetric key + record CID + nonce |\n| **write-cap** | PDS repo signing key (existing AT Proto property; DID-bound) |\n| **storage grid** | AT Proto PDS + IPFS (already existing substrate) |\n| **share token distribution** | Signal session between sender DID and recipient DID |\n\n## Record encoding\n\nEncrypted records use lexicon `com.etzhayyim.encrypted.record`. The payload is the AEAD ciphertext envelope:\n\n```\nEncryptedRecord {\n  v:        1                       // envelope version\n  alg:      \"xchacha20poly1305\"     // AEAD construction\n  nonce:    Uint8Array(24)          // unique per record\n  ciphertext: Uint8Array            // AEAD(key, nonce, aad=cid_self_ref) over CBOR(plaintext)\n  keyId:    string                  // hash prefix of sym_key for keywrap lookup\n  sender:   string                  // sender DID\n  createdAt: datetime\n}\n```\n\n- **Plaintext format**: arbitrary CBOR object (typically a record of an inner lexicon — e.g. `app.openmail.message` or a governance proposal).\n- **AAD binds the ciphertext to its CID** to prevent record-swap attacks within the same MST.\n- **CID is computed over the encrypted envelope**, so MST integrity proofs and L2 anchor finality apply unchanged.\n\n## Key distribution\n\nSymmetric keys are wrapped per-recipient via Signal session and stored in lexicon `com.etzhayyim.encrypted.keyWrap`:\n\n```\nKeyWrap {\n  v:          1\n  keyId:      string      // matches EncryptedRecord.keyId\n  sender:     string      // sender DID\n  recipient:  string      // recipient DID\n  ciphertext: Uint8Array  // libsignal-encrypted (sym_key)\n  signalSessionId: string // session identifier (recipient PDS-resolvable)\n  createdAt:  datetime\n}\n```\n\n- One `KeyWrap` record per (record, recipient) pair. Recipient enumerates `com.etzhayyim.encrypted.keyWrap` in their own PDS to discover keys they have read-cap for.\n- For group encryption: one `EncryptedRecord` + N `KeyWrap` records (one per group member). At low-thousands group size this is acceptable; at ~10⁴+ revisit with MLS.\n- Sender MAY also wrap to themselves (for self-decrypt later from a new device).\n\n## DID ↔ Signal identity binding\n\nPer actor (DID), exactly one Signal identity key. Binding is published as a signed assertion in the actor's PDS under lexicon `com.etzhayyim.encrypted.signalIdentity`:\n\n```\nSignalIdentity {\n  did:                 string  // self\n  signalIdentityKey:   Uint8Array  // libsignal IdentityKey (32 bytes Curve25519 pub)\n  signalRegistrationId: integer\n  signature:           Uint8Array  // sig by DID's signing key over the above\n  createdAt:           datetime\n}\n```\n\n- Resolvers (other actors' SDK instances) MUST verify the signature against the DID document's signing key before establishing a Signal session. This stops a malicious PDS from substituting a different Signal identity.\n- Rotation: publish a new record with new `createdAt`; latest wins. Old sessions can still decrypt past key-wraps (Signal session keys remain valid).\n\n## SDK seam (hard rule)\n\nApp code uses only `@etzhayyim/sdk`:\n\n```typescript\n// Write encrypted record + key-wraps for each recipient in one call.\nawait e.encryptedWrite({\n  collection: \"com.etzhayyim.encrypted.record\",\n  innerType:  \"com.etzhayyim.governance.proposal\",\n  record:     proposalBody,\n  recipients: [\"did:web:alice.example\", \"did:web:bob.example\"],\n});\n\n// Read (auto-decrypt if read-cap discoverable in own PDS keyWrap collection).\nconst { records } = await e.encryptedRead<ProposalBody>({\n  collection: \"com.etzhayyim.encrypted.record\",\n  innerType:  \"com.etzhayyim.governance.proposal\",\n});\n```\n\n- `@etzhayyim/sdk/crypto` wraps `@noble/ciphers` (audited pure-TS AEAD).\n- `@etzhayyim/sdk/signal` wraps `@signalapp/libsignal-client` (official Rust-binding).\n- `@etzhayyim/sdk/did-signal` implements DID↔Signal binding verification.\n- Direct app imports of `@noble/ciphers` / `@signalapp/libsignal-client` / `libsodium` / `tweetnacl` are **prohibited** by extending the ADR-2605172000 substrate hard-rules grep.\n\n# Consequences\n\n## 正の効果\n\n- **Private records on the existing substrate** without introducing a second storage system. PDS, IPFS, L2 anchor, SDK shape all reused.\n- **Verifier-from-outside preserved.** Third party can prove \"this ciphertext was committed at L2 block N\" without read-cap. Audit-without-reading is supported (e.g., a court order can compel the read-cap holder to disclose, but cannot rewrite history).\n- **Forward secrecy for key-wrap delivery** via Signal Double Ratchet — compromise of a Signal session key does not retroactively decrypt past key-wraps.\n- **DID-bound identity.** No \"Signal phone number\" exists; identity is the actor's DID, eliminating the discoverability footgun of Signal Messenger.\n- **Battle-tested cryptography.** Signal protocol + XChaCha20-Poly1305 + Curve25519 — no novel constructions.\n- **Group support up to etzhayyim's natural scale ceiling** (low thousands).\n\n## 負の効果 / コスト\n\n- **Metadata leakage that this design does not solve:**\n  - PDS sees **which DID wrote which CID at which time** and **ciphertext size**.\n  - MST keys (`collection/rkey`) are plaintext: leak structure and write order.\n  - PDS sees the **fan-out shape** of key-wraps (recipient set is visible as set of DIDs).\n  - Mitigations are deferred to a follow-up ADR: rkey blinding (`hash(sym_key, seq)`), ciphertext padding to fixed buckets, Sealed Sender for keyWrap authorship, optional Tor/onion PDS reads.\n- **Forward secrecy of the data itself is limited.** Symmetric record keys are static after creation; a key-wrap compromise reveals all past records using that key. Mitigation: per-channel or per-time-window rekey + \"logical delete\" via tombstone record + republish under new key. AT Proto's append-only MST means physical deletion is impossible.\n- **Group rekey on member removal** is manual: sender writes new `EncryptedRecord` with fresh `sym_key`, key-wraps to remaining members only. Removed member's read-cap on prior records persists (cannot be revoked once distributed).\n- **No PCS (post-compromise security) for records.** Signal Double Ratchet provides PCS for the wire (key-wrap delivery), but the symmetric data key, once leaked, stays leaked. MLS would not change this materially; PCS for at-rest data requires re-encryption schemes that are out of scope.\n- **One `KeyWrap` record per (record, recipient)** inflates PDS storage by O(group size). At ~10³ members per group this is fine; at 10⁵ it would be painful.\n- **SDK API surface grows.** Two new methods (`encryptedWrite`, `encryptedRead`), one new auth surface (Signal session lifecycle), and a key-rotation operational concept.\n- **libsignal native dependency.** `@signalapp/libsignal-client` ships a native module (Rust → N-API). Adds a build-time dependency on a working toolchain on each platform. Browser usage requires the WASM build path (`@signalapp/libsignal-client-wasm` or equivalent), tracked separately.\n\n## Rollout\n\n1. **This commit** — ADR + lexicons + SDK scaffold (crypto.ts, signal.ts, did-signal.ts) with `not yet implemented` stubs; vitest scaffolding.\n2. **Reference impl (next)** — `60-apps/open-isco/kotoba/` is public-only and not a fit. Use the council deliberation flow for the uhl-right-neural triage cohort (ADR-2605181050) as the first encrypted-record consumer.\n3. **Metadata-leakage follow-up ADR** — once a real consumer exists, decide on rkey blinding strategy and padding bucket sizes based on observed traffic shape.\n4. **Forward-secrecy follow-up ADR** — design the rekey + tombstone protocol after first six months of operational data.\n5. **MLS revisit trigger** — when a single encrypted group exceeds 10⁴ active members, or when `@signalapp/libsignal-client` upstream support degrades.\n\n# Alternatives Considered\n\n## A. Tahoe-LAFS as a separate substrate\n\nAdds a second storage system (Tahoe grid) that the substrate would need to operate alongside MST+IPFS+L2. Loses the property that all etzhayyim state has a single verifiable timeline anchored to L2. Operationally requires a Tahoe-grid operator — re-introduces a centralized trust point that ADR-2605172000 worked to eliminate.\n\n## B. MLS (RFC 9420)\n\nCryptographically stronger for groups (O(log N) sender ops, tree-based PCS, group rekey on removal). Rejected for v1 because production-grade implementations are young (`openmls` first prod-stable cut in 2024; AWS MLS used internally but limited public deployment history). At etzhayyim scale Signal's costs are not the bottleneck, so the trade favors the more-battle-tested protocol. Re-evaluate when scale or libsignal support changes.\n\n## C. Naive AES-GCM with public per-actor symmetric key\n\nPre-share a symmetric key per actor pair out-of-band, encrypt records with AES-GCM under that key. Trivial implementation but no forward secrecy, no key rotation story, no group support, and no formal cryptographic backing for the key-distribution side. Rejected.\n\n## D. age + manual key-wrap\n\nUse `age` (X25519 + ChaCha20-Poly1305) for record encryption with `age` recipients as the wrap mechanism. Simpler than Signal, but no session concept → no forward secrecy and no ratcheting; every recipient-add requires re-wrapping all past records (or accepting that they cannot be decrypted by the new member). Useful as a one-shot encryption for blobs at rest, but not for ongoing communications.\n\n## E. End-to-end on the wire only (Signal Messenger model)\n\nDon't persist anything; messages are ephemeral. Rejected because the substrate's audit/governance properties require durable records (council decisions, financial transactions, medical referrals all need a verifiable timeline).\n\n# References\n\n- ADR-2605172000 [etzhayyim/root kotoba substrate](./2605172000-etzhayyim-kotoba-substrate.md) — substrate this ADR extends\n- ADR-2605172100 [etzhayyim payments on-chain only](./2605172100-etzhayyim-payments-on-chain-only.md) — sibling hard-rule pattern\n- ADR-2605172200 [openmail atproto-MST SMTP bridge](./2605172200-openmail-atproto-mst-smtp-bridge.md) — first candidate consumer for private mail\n- ADR-2605181050 [UHL overseas referral paths](./2605181050-uhl-overseas-referral-paths.md) — drives private medical-records requirement\n- Signal protocol — X3DH https://signal.org/docs/specifications/x3db/ + Double Ratchet https://signal.org/docs/specifications/doubleratchet/\n- libsignal — https://github.com/signalapp/libsignal\n- XChaCha20-Poly1305 — RFC draft-irtf-cfrg-xchacha\n- MLS — RFC 9420 (rejected for v1; tracked for revisit)\n- Tahoe-LAFS — capability-based storage model https://tahoe-lafs.org/\n- AT Protocol MST spec — https://atproto.com/specs/repository\n", :adr/path "90-docs/adr/2605181100-mst-encrypted-records-signal-keywrap.edn", :adr/source-repo "root", :adr/topic "mst-encrypted-records-signal-keywrap", :adr/last_verified "2026-05-18", :adr/date "2026-05-18", :adr/authoritative true, :adr/related ["adr-2605172100-etzhayyim-payments-on-chain-only" "adr-2605172200-openmail-atproto-mst-smtp-bridge"], :adr/authoritative_for ["hard rule: private records on MST are XChaCha20-Poly1305 AEAD ciphertext, CID over ciphertext" "hard rule: per-record symmetric keys are wrapped per-recipient via Signal session" "hard rule: DID ↔ Signal identity binding is a signed assertion in the actor's PDS repo" "lexicon namespace: com.etzhayyim.encrypted.*" "SDK seam: @etzhayyim/sdk crypto + signal modules; apps MUST NOT import @noble/ciphers or @signalapp/libsignal-client directly" "rejection of MLS for v1 (revisit when libsignal scale ceiling hits)"]}]
